      "eventType": "goat.created",
      "producer": "identity admin goat create / procurement accepted-intake PC handoff",
      "producerFiles": [
        "backend/internal/identity/adapters/postgres/admin_goat_create.go",
        "backend/internal/procurement/adapters/postgres/goat_created_outbox.go"
      ],
      "consumers": [
        {
          "module": "vaccination",
          "file": "backend/internal/vaccination/app/generation_handler.go",
          "notes": "GoatCreatedHandler.HandleEvent reads the payload key trusted_vaccination_history (written by the procurement producer) and routes it through GenerationService.ingestPreArrivalHistory (backend/internal/vaccination/app/prearrival_history.go), which reviews each supplier claim and persists it as accepted or rejected pre-arrival history before generation runs (BUG-017)."
        },
        {
          "module": "tasks",
          "file": "backend/internal/tasks/app/event_handlers.go",
          "notes": "GoatCreatedWorkflowHandler.HandleEvent filters origin_type=birth, reads the canonical goats row (dob/time_of_birth/sex/park/shed) by PK, and opens the birth_kid workflow linked to the canonical mother plus the shared birth_mother workflow. For new births the identity producer resolves the scanned mother RFID before approval, persists mother_goat_id + litter_size in goat_births atomically with kid creation, and emits that canonical UUID as dam_id; unresolved dam handling remains only for legacy events. The consumer parses dob, dam_id, sex, and time_of_birth; the tasks repository uses that canonical event moment to include remaining birth-day colostrum slots before their 15-minute cutoff plus all five next-day slots, so none of those event keys is accept-and-discard. Each scheduled round is hard-gated until its stored due_at and then remains available; Tag the kid waits for every other kid task, including the entire scheduled series."
        }
      ],
      "owningDoc": "context/architecture/domain-event-integration-contract.md",
